#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184033 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3146eb6-b4ef-554d-83c1-351a3fb15651" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Terms of the Taylor Series of a Function About a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "ef6bfc82-5993-514e-bbb4-6b3769bec0e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-752" :question/problem "Find the first four non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $e^{-x}$ centered about the point $x=3$." :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = f(a) + f'(a)(x-a) + \\frac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2 +\\frac{f'''(a)}{3!}(x-a)^3+\\cdots\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nf(x)=e^{-x}\n$$\n\nand $a=3$.\n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= e^{-x} \\\\\nf(3) &= e^{-3} \\\\\nf^{′}(x) &= -e^{-x} \\\\\nf^{′}(3) &= -e^{-3} \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= e^{-x} \\\\\nf^{″}(3) &= e^{-3} \\\\\nf^{‴}(x) &= -e^{-x} \\\\\nf^{‴}(3) &= -e^{-3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{-x} &= e^{-3} + (-e^{-3})(x - 3) + ((e^{-3}))/(2!)(x - 3)^{2} + ((-e^{-3}))/(3!)(x - 3)^{3} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= e^{-3} - e^{-3}(x - 3) + \\frac{e^{-3}}{2}(x - 3)^{2} - \\frac{e^{-3}}{6}(x - 3)^{3} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184034 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series"}] :question/id #uuid "e82fb0f9-372a-50ab-a6a5-c1dc8868a2d6" :question/math-academy-id "e-753" :question/problem "Use the first three terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x) = \\sqrt x$ about $x=9$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{9.1}$." :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = f(a) + f'(a)(x-a) + \\frac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2 +\\cdots\n$$\n\nwith $a=9$.\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= x^{1/2} \\\\\nf(9) &= 3 \\\\\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{1}{2}x^{-1/2} \\\\\nf^{′}(9) &= \\frac{1}{6} \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= -\\frac{1}{4}x^{-3/2} \\\\\nf^{″}(9) &= -\\frac{1}{108}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{x} &= 3 + \\frac{1}{6}(x - 9) + \\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{108})(x - 9)^{2} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 3 + \\frac{1}{6}(x - 9) - \\frac{1}{216}(x - 9)^{2} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x = 9.1$ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{9.1}$. We get\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{9.1} \\mid \\approx 3 + \\frac{1}{6}(9.1 - 9) - \\frac{1}{216}(9.1 - 9)^{2}; \\approx 3 + \\frac{1}{6}(0.1) - \\frac{1}{216}(0.1)^{2}; = 3.016620\n$$\n\nto six decimal places.\n\n**Note:** The exact value of $\\sqrt{9.1}$ is $3.016\\,621$ to six decimal places. So, our approximation is very good!"}]}
